1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:05,403
Every time you ask a chatbot a question, something happens that you never see.

2
00:00:05,853 --> 00:00:09,107
Somewhere, in a building the size of several football pitches,

3
00:00:09,107 --> 00:00:11,154
thousands of chips run a little hotter.

4
00:00:11,504 --> 00:00:13,602
Pumps push coolant through the racks.

5
00:00:13,602 --> 00:00:16,947
And beyond the fence line, a power grid strains to keep up.

6
00:00:17,547 --> 00:00:21,396
We talk about artificial intelligence as if it were weightless.

7
00:00:21,396 --> 00:00:22,984
Software. Code. The cloud.

8
00:00:23,484 --> 00:00:27,817
But the AI boom runs on concrete, copper, steel and electricity.

9
00:00:28,317 --> 00:00:30,614
And some of its biggest winners make nothing

10
00:00:30,614 --> 00:00:32,911
you would ever call artificial intelligence.

11
00:00:39,911 --> 00:00:42,519
Follow the money, and it starts with four companies.

12
00:00:42,919 --> 00:00:47,197
In their latest financial years, Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon and Meta

13
00:00:47,197 --> 00:00:51,971
together spent more than four hundred billion dollars on capital expenditure.

14
00:00:52,321 --> 00:00:56,032
And they are accelerating. Amazon alone expects to spend

15
00:00:56,032 --> 00:00:59,610
around two hundred and twenty billion dollars in 2026.

16
00:01:00,010 --> 00:01:04,648
Most of that money pays for one thing: data centres, filled with AI chips.

17
00:01:05,848 --> 00:01:09,208
To see who captures that money, picture AI as a stack.

18
00:01:09,608 --> 00:01:12,696
At the top, the applications and models. Below them,

19
00:01:12,696 --> 00:01:15,666
the chips. Then the factories that make the chips.

20
00:01:15,966 --> 00:01:19,979
The data centres that house them. The power stations that feed them.

21
00:01:19,979 --> 00:01:22,930
The lines and substations that deliver that power.

22
00:01:23,230 --> 00:01:25,449
And at the very bottom, the transformers,

23
00:01:25,449 --> 00:01:28,804
switchgear and cables that make all of it physically possible.

24
00:01:29,304 --> 00:01:33,437
Most of the attention sits at the top. This story goes all the way down.

25
00:01:35,437 --> 00:01:37,469
The most visible winner is Nvidia.

26
00:01:37,819 --> 00:01:40,373
In the three months to October 2026,

27
00:01:40,373 --> 00:01:45,340
it reported revenue of almost one hundred and sixteen billion dollars.

28
00:01:45,640 --> 00:01:48,859
More than ninety percent of it came from data centres.

29
00:01:48,859 --> 00:01:53,091
And it kept roughly seventy-five cents of every dollar as gross profit.

30
00:01:53,541 --> 00:01:56,855
But Nvidia does not make its own chips. It designs them.

31
00:01:57,655 --> 00:02:03,109
Manufacturing goes to Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company, or TSMC.

32
00:02:03,409 --> 00:02:07,468
By one industry estimate, it took nearly three-quarters of all

33
00:02:07,468 --> 00:02:11,265
contract chipmaking revenue in the second quarter of 2026.

34
00:02:11,565 --> 00:02:15,828
High-performance computing now makes up about two-thirds of its sales.

35
00:02:15,828 --> 00:02:18,813
And it has told investors it plans to spend up to

36
00:02:18,813 --> 00:02:22,041
sixty-four billion dollars on new capacity this year.

37
00:02:22,541 --> 00:02:25,958
TSMC, in turn, relies on a single Dutch company.

38
00:02:26,258 --> 00:02:29,843
ASML is the world's only maker of the extreme ultraviolet

39
00:02:29,843 --> 00:02:33,554
lithography machines used to print the most advanced chips.

40
00:02:33,854 --> 00:02:38,014
The newest models are reported to cost hundreds of millions of dollars each.

41
00:02:38,514 --> 00:02:42,597
Then there is memory. SK hynix makes the high-bandwidth memory

42
00:02:42,597 --> 00:02:46,284
stacked beside each AI processor. In its latest quarter,

43
00:02:46,284 --> 00:02:50,038
it reported an operating margin of around eighty percent.

44
00:02:50,438 --> 00:02:55,304
And the finished pieces must be bonded together, in a process called advanced

45
00:02:55,304 --> 00:02:59,790
packaging. Another bottleneck, and another place TSMC is pouring money.

46
00:03:00,790 --> 00:03:02,803
Each link captures value differently.

47
00:03:03,103 --> 00:03:05,543
The designer earns the richest margins.

48
00:03:05,543 --> 00:03:10,735
The manufacturer carries the heaviest capital bill. The toolmaker holds a monopoly.

49
00:03:11,135 --> 00:03:13,753
But all of them depend on the same customers,

50
00:03:13,753 --> 00:03:16,778
and those customers are building something enormous.

51
00:03:18,478 --> 00:03:22,658
A chip is only useful inside a building that can power it and cool it.

52
00:03:23,008 --> 00:03:28,392
And an AI data centre is not a warehouse of computers. It is an industrial machine.

53
00:03:28,842 --> 00:03:34,014
Nvidia's latest rack-scale systems are specified at around one hundred and twenty

54
00:03:34,014 --> 00:03:39,058
kilowatts per rack. That's many times what a typical server rack was built for.

55
00:03:39,358 --> 00:03:41,765
Air can no longer carry that much heat away.

56
00:03:41,765 --> 00:03:44,008
So liquid is piped straight to the chips.

57
00:03:44,808 --> 00:03:48,480
That has turned cooling and power equipment into a growth industry.

58
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:52,910
Vertiv, which makes both, reported a sixty-one percent jump in sales

59
00:03:52,910 --> 00:03:56,858
in its latest quarter, and said organic orders more than doubled.

60
00:03:57,158 --> 00:03:59,615
Eaton, which makes electrical equipment,

61
00:03:59,615 --> 00:04:03,792
says the backlog in its electrical business is up forty-six percent.

62
00:04:04,242 --> 00:04:09,028
McKinsey estimates that of the money going into AI data centres this decade,

63
00:04:09,028 --> 00:04:11,988
about sixty percent goes on chips and hardware,

64
00:04:11,988 --> 00:04:15,955
and about a quarter on power, cooling and electrical equipment.

65
00:04:16,455 --> 00:04:21,117
And space is still scarce. In North America's main markets,

66
00:04:21,117 --> 00:04:26,412
the property firm CBRE puts vacancy at a record low of 1.4 percent.

67
00:04:26,712 --> 00:04:30,105
Some of it is being built on borrowed money. CoreWeave,

68
00:04:30,105 --> 00:04:34,610
which rents out Nvidia chips, reports a contract backlog of more than one

69
00:04:34,610 --> 00:04:39,237
hundred and thirty billion dollars, and debt of around forty-seven billion.

70
00:04:40,837 --> 00:04:44,364
Which brings us to the problem that no one can code their way around.

71
00:04:44,964 --> 00:04:45,840
Electricity.

72
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:51,299
In 2024, the world's data centres used around four hundred and fifteen

73
00:04:51,299 --> 00:04:56,423
terawatt-hours of electricity. About one and a half percent of global demand.

74
00:04:56,723 --> 00:05:01,511
The International Energy Agency expects that to more than double by 2030.

75
00:05:01,811 --> 00:05:05,238
In the United States, data centres are set to account for

76
00:05:05,238 --> 00:05:08,845
nearly half of all growth in electricity demand this decade.

77
00:05:09,645 --> 00:05:12,132
First, the power has to be generated.

78
00:05:12,132 --> 00:05:16,031
Big Tech is now signing deals to restart nuclear reactors.

79
00:05:16,331 --> 00:05:19,963
Microsoft agreed to buy the output of Three Mile Island's

80
00:05:19,963 --> 00:05:23,212
Unit One for twenty years, if it comes back online.

81
00:05:23,612 --> 00:05:28,518
Gas turbines are in such demand that GE Vernova's gas backlog and reserved

82
00:05:28,518 --> 00:05:32,960
production slots have reached one hundred and twenty-two gigawatts.

83
00:05:33,460 --> 00:05:37,611
Then the power has to be delivered. And here, the bottleneck is physical.

84
00:05:38,011 --> 00:05:41,757
Berkeley Lab found that the typical American power project

85
00:05:41,757 --> 00:05:45,761
built in 2025 waited around five years to connect to the grid.

86
00:05:46,161 --> 00:05:48,629
And every connection needs transformers.

87
00:05:48,629 --> 00:05:53,072
A large power transformer can weigh hundreds of tonnes, and the wait for

88
00:05:53,072 --> 00:05:57,329
one is commonly two years or more. For the largest units, up to four.

89
00:05:57,629 --> 00:06:01,831
Prices have climbed too: up sixty to eighty percent since 2020,

90
00:06:01,831 --> 00:06:03,698
according to Wood Mackenzie.

91
00:06:04,698 --> 00:06:07,508
This is where some of the quietest fortunes are being made.

92
00:06:07,908 --> 00:06:13,539
GE Vernova's order backlog has reached one hundred and ninety-seven billion dollars.

93
00:06:13,539 --> 00:06:18,701
Siemens Energy's stands at a record one hundred and sixty-four billion euros.

94
00:06:19,001 --> 00:06:23,116
Quanta Services, which builds power lines and substations,

95
00:06:23,116 --> 00:06:27,231
has a record backlog of nearly sixty-four billion dollars.

96
00:06:27,231 --> 00:06:31,204
And cable maker Prysmian posted record earnings in 2025.

97
00:06:31,604 --> 00:06:33,402
They do not sell intelligence.

98
00:06:33,402 --> 00:06:37,057
They sell the physical capacity that intelligence depends on.

99
00:06:38,857 --> 00:06:40,200
So who actually wins?

100
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:44,009
The chip designers capture the richest margins today,

101
00:06:44,009 --> 00:06:47,521
but they depend on a handful of buyers. Earlier this year,

102
00:06:47,521 --> 00:06:51,880
three direct customers accounted for more than half of Nvidia's revenue.

103
00:06:52,230 --> 00:06:57,137
The foundries and toolmakers hold positions that are almost impossible to copy,

104
00:06:57,137 --> 00:07:01,174
but they must keep investing tens of billions just to stay ahead.

105
00:07:01,524 --> 00:07:04,180
The cloud giants carry the biggest bill of all,

106
00:07:04,180 --> 00:07:08,193
and investors are watching how much of that spending turns into profit.

107
00:07:08,543 --> 00:07:11,251
The grid's equipment makers have something rarer:

108
00:07:11,251 --> 00:07:14,954
order books that stretch for years, from customers who cannot wait.

109
00:07:15,354 --> 00:07:19,516
And someone has to pay. On America's largest grid, run by PJM,

110
00:07:19,516 --> 00:07:25,019
the price of capacity has jumped more than tenfold in three years. Its independent

111
00:07:25,019 --> 00:07:30,523
market monitor attributes more than twenty-three billion dollars of added capacity

112
00:07:30,523 --> 00:07:35,826
costs to data centre demand. Costs that are passed on to electricity customers.

113
00:07:36,276 --> 00:07:41,394
None of this guarantees the future. Backlogs can shrink. Forecasts can be wrong.

114
00:07:41,394 --> 00:07:45,041
And capacity built for a boom can sit idle in a downturn.

115
00:07:46,941 --> 00:07:50,987
The AI revolution is usually told as a story about software.

116
00:07:51,437 --> 00:07:54,164
But follow the money, and it leads somewhere else.

117
00:07:54,164 --> 00:07:57,600
To chip plants in Taiwan and machine makers in the Netherlands.

118
00:07:57,900 --> 00:08:02,892
To cooling plants and cable mills. To turbines, transformers and power lines.

119
00:08:03,392 --> 00:08:07,553
The intelligence may be artificial. The infrastructure is very real.

120
00:08:08,153 --> 00:08:12,600
And the companies building it may turn out to be the hidden winners of the AI boom.
